---
// web/src/pages/legal/disclaimers/index.astro
// /legal/disclaimers/ (Phase 5 Step 16; ROADMAP §5 "pyeconomics' own licence,
// name and posture": "Disclaimers on every surface"; ADR-0013 decision 20):
// educational and informational use only; not personal advice; not registered
// with any regulator; as is, with the limit of liability; models and data that
// can be wrong, delayed or revised; the hypothetical-results legend (FINRA
// Rule 2214's as the template, CFTC Rule 4.41(b)(1)'s statement for futures)
// and the models that carry it, generated from their registry tags; the data
// notices; AI assistants; referral links; and the CFA® marks notice.
import Breadcrumbs from "@/components/Breadcrumbs.astro";
import Base from "@/layouts/Base.astro";
import {
  CFTC_STATEMENT,
  CONTACT_EMAIL,
  DISCLAIMERS,
  FINRA_2214_LEGEND,
  FUTURES_TAGS,
  HYPOTHETICAL_LEGEND,
  LEGAL_INDEX,
  SIMULATED_TAGS,
  simulates,
} from "@/lib/legal";
import { AS_IS_NOTICE, CFA_NOTICE } from "@/lib/links";
import { modelPath } from "@/lib/paths";
import { parseModels } from "@/lib/registry-schema";
import { registryData } from "@/lib/site-data";
import { webPage, type Crumb } from "@/lib/structured-data";

const trail: Crumb[] = [
  { name: "Home", path: "/" },
  { name: LEGAL_INDEX.title, path: LEGAL_INDEX.path },
  { name: DISCLAIMERS.title, path: DISCLAIMERS.path },
];
const simulated = parseModels(registryData)
  .filter(simulates)
  .sort((a, b) => a.title.localeCompare(b.title));
const h2 = "mt-10 text-2xl font-semibold";
const p = "mt-3 max-w-3xl";
const quote = "mt-3 max-w-3xl border-l-4 pl-4";
---

<Base
  title={DISCLAIMERS.title}
  description={DISCLAIMERS.description}
  jsonLd={webPage(DISCLAIMERS.path, DISCLAIMERS.title, DISCLAIMERS.description, trail)}
  searchable
>
  <Breadcrumbs trail={trail} />
  <span hidden data-pagefind-meta="kind:Legal"></span>
  <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight">{DISCLAIMERS.title}</h1>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-muted-foreground">Last updated {DISCLAIMERS.updated}</p>
  <p class="mt-4 max-w-3xl text-lg font-medium" data-notice="as-is">{AS_IS_NOTICE}</p>

  <h2 id="education" class={h2}>Education and information only</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    pyeconomics is for educational and informational use only. Nothing on this site, from its API or MCP endpoint, in
    the Python package, the command line or the local app, or in anything they return, is investment, financial, tax,
    legal or accounting advice, or a recommendation or offer to buy, sell or hold any security, futures contract or
    other instrument.
  </p>

  <h2 id="not-personal" class={h2}>Not personal advice</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    pyeconomics publishes general tools: formula calculators, pricing models, regressions, simulations of rules you
    set, and charts. They give every user the same function and know nothing of your circumstances, objectives or risk
    tolerance. Before acting on any result, consider whether it suits you, and seek advice from a qualified professional
    who knows your situation.
  </p>

  <h2 id="not-registered" class={h2}>Not registered with any regulator</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    pyeconomics is not registered with any securities, commodities or other financial regulator, in any country, and
    offers no service that would require registration: it manages no money, executes no trades, routes no orders and
    gives no recommendations.
  </p>

  <h2 id="as-is" class={h2}>As is, with a limit of liability</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    The software, the service, their results and their data are provided as is, without warranty of any kind, and our
    liability is limited, as the <a href="/legal/terms/#no-warranty">terms of use</a> and the
    <a href="/legal/licence/">licence</a> set out.
  </p>

  <h2 id="models-and-data" class={h2}>Models and data can be wrong</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    Every model rests on assumptions and has limitations, which its page states with its sources. A model can be wrong,
    or unsuitable for a particular purpose, and a correct model gives wrong answers from wrong inputs. Verify results
    independently before you rely on them.
  </p>
  <p class={p}>
    Data can be delayed, revised, incomplete or wrong. This site's data comes from a daily snapshot, so it can be up to a
    day old, and older when a source is down; a result then carries a stale-data warning. Sources revise what they
    publish, and a result marks where it read revised data rather than the data known on a past date.
  </p>

  <h2 id="hypothetical-results" class={h2}>Hypothetical results</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    Some models simulate: they draw many possible paths, replay history or test a rule against the past. Their outputs
    are hypothetical. Wherever a model's results are simulated or hypothetical, its page shows this legend beside them,
    which follows the wording of FINRA Rule 2214(c)(4):
  </p>
  <blockquote class={quote} data-legend="hypothetical">
    <p class="font-semibold">{HYPOTHETICAL_LEGEND}</p>
  </blockquote>
  <p class={p}>
    FINRA's text, which pyeconomics uses as a template and is not bound by, reads: "{FINRA_2214_LEGEND}" Results may also
    vary with each use and over time, as the inputs, the random seed and the data change.
  </p>
  <p class={p}>
    A model that simulates the performance of futures or other commodity interests also carries the statement CFTC Rule
    4.41(b)(1)(i) prescribes:
  </p>
  <blockquote class={quote} data-legend="cftc">
    <p>{CFTC_STATEMENT}</p>
  </blockquote>
  <p class={p}>
    A model carries the legend when its tags include {SIMULATED_TAGS.map((tag) => `"${tag}"`).join(", ")}, and the CFTC
    statement too when they also include {FUTURES_TAGS.map((tag) => `"${tag}"`).join(" or ")}. Today these models
    carry the legend:
  </p>
  <ul class="mt-3 max-w-3xl list-disc pl-6" data-legend-models="">
    {
      simulated.map((model) => (
        <li data-legend-model={model.id}>
          <a href={modelPath(model.id)}>{model.title}</a> (<code>{model.id}</code>)
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>

  <h2 id="data-notices" class={h2}>Data notices</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    Every chart, table and file carries its data's attribution, its source's required notices and a link to the
    source's terms. The sources do not endorse pyeconomics. <a href="/sources/">Data sources and licences</a> lists each
    source's notices, and the <a href="/legal/terms/#data">terms of use</a> pass on the conditions the New York Fed and
    the OECD attach to their data.
  </p>

  <h2 id="assistants" class={h2}>AI assistants</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    An AI assistant that uses pyeconomics through its MCP server runs the same models and reads the same data, but what
    the assistant then says is its own, not pyeconomics'. An assistant can misread a result or present it as advice it
    is not. pyeconomics offers no tool that answers what to buy or sell.
  </p>

  <h2 id="referrals" class={h2}>Referral links</h2>
  <p class={p}>pyeconomics has no referral or affiliate links. If it ever adds one, the link will say so.</p>

  <h2 id="trademarks" class={h2}>Trademarks</h2>
  <p class={p}>
    pyeconomics implements formulas commonly covered in the CFA® Program curriculum and maps its models to the
    Program's topic areas as factual tags; it copies no curriculum content.
  </p>
  <p class={p} data-notice="cfa-disclaimer">{CFA_NOTICE}</p>
  <p class={p}>
    FRED® and ALFRED® are registered trademarks of the Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis. Other names are the marks of
    their owners, used only to name their data. pyeconomics is not the GitHub project davidrpugh/pyeconomics or the site
    pyecon.org.
  </p>

  <h2 id="contact" class={h2}>Contact</h2>
  <p class={p}>Questions about these disclaimers: <a href={`mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}`}>{CONTACT_EMAIL}</a>.</p>
</Base>
